//! run
//! stdout:
//| true false
//| false false false
//| true true
//| true true
//| true false
//| true true
//| true false
//| false
// A comparison of two untyped operands, one of which folded to no value. The
// comparison yields bool, so no context reaches its operands: the rule of the
// default type answers for them where the comparison is checked, and it
// answers over both sides together, because one type covers one untyped
// expression (D6.2, and the notes of 2026-09-13 and 2026-09-14 on D4.5).
//
// The pair took the left operand's type until 2026-09-14, and the other
// operand kept the type it had provisionally. Every line below was a wrong
// answer or a module clang refused, in both compilers.
// fail/constants/014_a_comparison_of_two_untyped_operands.ft holds the half of
// the rule that is an error.
//
// `one`, `two` and `thirtyone` come through a call, so `1 << one` folds to no
// value and the comparison cannot fold either.
//
// Line 1: one side asks for i64 and the other does not, so the pair is i64.
// `2147483648 > (1 << one)` gave the constant i64 and the shift i32 before,
// which clang refused as two widths in one comparison; the reversed form
// printed `true`, where 2 > 2147483648 is false.
// Line 2: neither side asks for more than i32, so the pair stays i32 and the
// answers are the ones it always gave.
// Line 3: the integer clause wins over the char clause, so the char literal is
// its code point and both operands are i32. The char on the left made an i8
// meet an i32 before.
// Line 4: the width the pair chose, read at run time. `<<` wraps at the width
// of its left operand (D6.2), so `1 << thirtyone` is 2147483648 in i64 and
// -2147483648 in i32: these two lines are `false` unless the constant on the
// other side carried the shift to i64.
// Line 5: the constant that needs i64 stands inside the expression that folded
// to no value, on either side.
// Line 6: the other end of i64, below i32's minimum.
// Line 7: a constant with no default type of its own that folds back into
// range. 2^63 is outside i64 and `2^63 >> 1` is not, and the value it carries
// asks for i64.
// Line 8: the width again, read through the shift count and not through the
// value. A count of 40 is legal for i64 and out of range for i32, and the
// count is checked at run time when it is a variable (D6.2), so the line
// prints an answer here and would abort with `shift count 40 out of range for
// i32` if the pair had left the shift at i32. The emitter writes the width
// into that check and into the message, so this line covers the trapping path
// that a count of 31 cannot reach.
fn z(i32 v) i32 {
    return v;
}

fn main() i32 {
    i32 one = z(1);
    i32 two = z(2);
    i32 thirtyone = z(31);
    i32 forty = z(40);
    println(2147483648 > (1 << one), " ", (1 << one) > 2147483648);
    println(2 > (1 << one), " ", (1 << one) > 2, " ", (1 << one) > (1 << two));
    println('a' > (1 << one), " ", (1 << one) < 'a');
    println((1 << thirtyone) == 2147483648, " ", 2147483648 == (1 << thirtyone));
    println((2147483648 << one) > 1, " ", 1 > (2147483648 << one));
    println(-2147483649 < (1 << one), " ", (1 << one) != -2147483649);
    println((9223372036854775808 >> 1) > (1 << one),
            " ",
            (1 << one) == (18446744073709551615 >> 32));
    println(2147483648 > (1 << forty));
    return 0;
}
